          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Period is your pride"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.064,
            0.103,
            0.242,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Menstruation is a sign of a healthy reproductive system, not something to be ashamed of. Using clean menstrual products, changing them as often as required, and disposing them responsibly helps in staying healthy and keeping your surroundings clean."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.088,
            0.616,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.5.7 Pregnancy and childbirth"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.283,
            0.11,
            0.89,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pregnancy in humans lasts about nine months and is divided into three stages called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "trimesters",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In the first trimester, the fertilised egg develops into an embryo during the first two months and major organs start forming during this time. From about the ninth week, the developing embryo is called a foetus, which continues to grow and develop. In the second trimester, the foetus grows bigger and stronger, and the mother can usually feel its movements. In the third trimester, the baby grows rapidly and gets ready for the life outside the womb (Figure 11.22). The uterus protects and nourishes the baby throughout this time. During childbirth, strong contractions of the muscles of the uterus help push the foetus out through the birth canal. In some cases, if a normal vaginal birth is not possible or safe for the mother or the foetus, doctors may use medical or surgical procedures to help deliver the baby safely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.469,
            0.289,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1st Trimester"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.469,
            0.562,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2nd Trimester"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.685,
            0.47,
            0.79,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3rd Trimester"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.493,
            0.63,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 11.22: Stages of pregnancy"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.515,
            0.89,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "After birth, a baby needs special care. Breastfeeding is essential because a mother’s milk provides complete nutrition and protects the infant from many diseases. Newborns must be kept warm, vaccinated on time and handled gently. The mother’s well-being is equally important. She requires nutritious food and adequate rest, and it is recommended for her to avoid harmful habits, such as smoking, consuming alcohol, or taking any medicines without medical advice. The health of the mother and the baby are closely connected, and require proper care and support."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
